{"name":"Symmetry","hex":"#8fa0a7","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"symmetry","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Exact correspondence on either side of a dividing line, plane, center or axis.","The satisfying arrangement of a balanced distribution of the elements of a whole."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/symmetry"}],"entries":[{"title":"Symmetry","blocks":[{"text":"In everyday life, symmetry refers to a sense of harmonious and beautiful proportion and balance. In mathematics, the term has a more precise definition and is usually used to refer to an object that is invariant under some transformations, such as translation, reflection, rotation, or scaling. Although these two meanings of the word can sometimes be told apart, they are intricately related, and hence are discussed together in this article."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/Asymmetric_%28PSF%29.svg/330px-Asymmetric_%28PSF%29.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symmetry","wikidata":"Q12485"},{"title":"Symmetry (physics)","blocks":[{"text":"A symmetry of a physical system is a physical or mathematical feature of the system that is preserved or remains unchanged under some transformation."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c1/Brillouin_Zone_%281st%2C_FCC%29.svg/330px-Brillouin_Zone_%281st%2C_FCC%29.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symmetry_(physics)","wikidata":"Q1455736"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3a/BigPlatoBig.png/500px-BigPlatoBig.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3a/BigPlatoBig.png/500px-BigPlatoBig.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A fractal-like shape that has reflectional symmetry, rotational symmetry and self-similarity, three forms of symmetry. This shape is obtained by a finite subdivision rule.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABigPlatoBig.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/Chance_and_a_Half%2C_Posing.jpg/500px-Chance_and_a_Half%2C_Posing.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/Chance_and_a_Half%2C_Posing.jpg/1280px-Chance_and_a_Half%2C_Posing.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/Chance_and_a_Half%2C_Posing.jpg/500px-Chance_and_a_Half%2C_Posing.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Many animals are approximately mirror-symmetric, though internal organs are often arranged asymmetrically.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AChance_and_a_Half%2C_Posing.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/19/Taj_Mahal%2C_Agra_views_from_around_%2885%29.JPG/500px-Taj_Mahal%2C_Agra_views_from_around_%2885%29.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/19/Taj_Mahal%2C_Agra_views_from_around_%2885%29.JPG/1280px-Taj_Mahal%2C_Agra_views_from_around_%2885%29.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/19/Taj_Mahal%2C_Agra_views_from_around_%2885%29.JPG/500px-Taj_Mahal%2C_Agra_views_from_around_%2885%29.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Seen from the side, the Taj Mahal has bilateral symmetry; from the top (in plan), it has fourfold symmetry.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATaj_Mahal%2C_Agra_views_from_around_(85).JPG"},{"srcset":"https://